---
import Pic from '../../components/Pic.astro';
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Assessment from '../../components/Assessment.astro';
import Hero from '../../components/Hero.astro';
import site from '../../data/site.json';
const page = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'WebPage',
  '@id': site.url + '/keynote-speaker/executive-decision-making-keynote', url: site.url + '/keynote-speaker/executive-decision-making-keynote',
  name: 'Decision-Making Keynote Speaker | Jack P. Flaherty',
  description: 'Fear Is Not A Control, a keynote for health-system leaders and professional services partners by Jack P. Flaherty. Treat new technology like a new hire.',
  about: { '@type': 'Thing', name: 'Technology decision-making' },
  author: { '@id': site.url + '/#person' }
};
---
<Base title="Decision-Making Keynote Speaker | Jack P. Flaherty"
  description="Fear Is Not A Control, a keynote for health-system leaders and professional services partners by Jack P. Flaherty. Treat new technology like a new hire."
  path="/keynote-speaker/executive-decision-making-keynote" image="/images/jack-p-flaherty-keynote-stage-wide.jpg" schema={[page]} hero="/images/jack-p-flaherty-keynote-stage-wide.jpg">

  <Hero image="/images/jack-p-flaherty-keynote-stage-wide.jpg" alt="Jack P. Flaherty delivering the Fear Is Not A Control keynote on a dark stage" pos="72% 50%">
    <h1>Fear Is Not A Control</h1>
    <p>A keynote for health-system leaders and professional services partners</p>
    <p>{site.position}</p>
    <p>A standing ovation is not the deliverable. The deliverable is a room that leaves with three questions to ask before any technology decision, and one first move for the week they get home.</p>
    <div class="btns"><a class="btn" href="/book-jack">Book Jack</a><a class="btn light" href="/#reel">Watch the reel</a></div>
  </Hero>

  <section class="tight">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="lede">Jack P. Flaherty has spent 25 years in risk and advisory, 15 of them at the Big Four, in the rooms where technology decisions get made and defended.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>The problem</h2>
      <p class="lede">Most keynotes are rented for the day. The insight leaves with the speaker.</p>
      <div class="grid c3" style="margin-top:1.5rem">
        <div class="card">
          <p class="kicker">01</p>
          <h3>Leaders are trained in everything except how to decide.</h3>
          <p>Organizations invest in strategy, culture and technology. Almost none invest in the one skill that determines whether any of it works: making a confident, well-informed call under pressure. The gap shows up as missed timelines, stalled initiatives and teams waiting for permission nobody is giving.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <p class="kicker">02</p>
          <h3>The decision was already made. Nobody in the room knew it.</h3>
          <p>By the time most technology decisions reach a meeting, the outcome is settled. A tool is in use, a vendor is signed, a system is live. The leaders in the room are ratifying, not deciding. The most dangerous decisions are the ones a team made without noticing, and fear made most of them.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <p class="kicker">03</p>
          <h3>The signals were always there. Nobody was assigned to watch.</h3>
          <p>Every major failure Jack has sat close to had early warning signs. The organizations that came through were not smarter. Someone had the job of reading what the others ignored. Decide, then watch for what moves.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="tight">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="pull">The teams that adopt new technology well are not the ones with the best plan. They are the ones who gave the decision an owner.</h2>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="split">
        <div>
          <h2>The keynote</h2>
          <p class="lede">One talk. Two rooms. Built for the decision in front of each.</p>
        </div>
        <p class="lede">The method is simple enough to repeat on the drive home: treat new technology like a new hire. A job description. A manager. A probation period. A review.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid c2" style="margin-top:2.5rem">
        <div class="card accent">
          <p class="kicker">For health-system executives and nurse leaders</p>
          <h3>Making the Call on New Technology When Patients Are Waiting</h3>
          <p>A new tool shows up on the unit. Half the team is using it by Friday. The other half waits for someone to say it is safe. Nobody owns the decision, so fear makes it.</p>
          <p>Jack has watched decision-making teams, not surgical teams, save a life. He served as acting enterprise risk and audit leader for a $10B health system and led a board-mandated cybersecurity program for a $4B health system that closed its urgent gaps on a $2.5 million budget within one year.</p>
          <p>The room leaves with the three questions to ask before any technology decision reaches a patient, and one first move for the week they get home. <a href="/keynote-speaker/fear-is-not-a-control-healthcare" title="Fear Is Not A Control, the healthcare keynote">See the healthcare keynote</a></p>
        </div>
        <div class="card accent">
          <p class="kicker">For partners in accounting, advisory and law firms</p>
          <h3>Making Technology Decisions Your Partners and Clients Will Stand Behind</h3>
          <p>A client asks what the firm thinks about the tool their team adopted last quarter. The partner has an opinion and no position. Every firm has a partner who owns the call on new technology, or it has fear making it.</p>
          <p>Jack spent 15 years at the Big Four, where he served as a director, and has presented at PwC's national Healthcare Risk Roundtable twice a year for five years. The firms he watched move first on new technology shared one trait: a partner who owned the call.</p>
          <p>The room leaves with the three questions to ask before any technology decision, in the firm or at a client, and one first move for the next partner meeting. <a href="/keynote-speaker/fear-is-not-a-control-professional-services" title="Fear Is Not A Control, the professional services keynote">See the professional services keynote</a></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="dark" id="the-decision-switch">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="split">
        <Pic src="jack-p-flaherty-decision-switch-stage-bw.jpg" alt="Jack P. Flaherty on stage delivering The Decision Switch keynote, in black and white" />
        <div>
          <p class="kicker">The original keynote. The one in the reel.</p>
          <h2>The Decision Switch</h2>
          <p>Most leaders do not lose to bad decisions. They lose to decisions that never got made. The meeting ends, the issue stays open, and three months later the cost shows up somewhere else.</p>
          <p>The Decision Switch is the method Jack built over 25 years in risk and advisory and wrote down in his book. Seven principles that turn a stalled decision into one the team will stand behind.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="grid c3" style="margin-top:2.5rem">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>What the room leaves with</h3>
          <ul class="checks">
            <li>A way to triage what is on the desk, so the decision that matters gets made first.</li>
            <li>How to bring the people who disagree into the decision instead of around it.</li>
            <li>A review habit that turns every outcome, good or bad, into the next better call.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Built for</h3>
          <ul class="checks">
            <li>Executive teams</li>
            <li>Leadership offsites</li>
            <li>Association conferences</li>
            <li>Any room that owns decisions and is tired of carrying them</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Format</h3>
          <ul class="checks">
            <li>45 to 60 minutes</li>
            <li>Live or virtual</li>
            <li>Built for the room after a discovery call</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="btns"><a class="btn light" href="/#reel" title="Watch the speaker reel">Watch the reel</a><a class="btn" href="/book-jack" title="Book The Decision Switch keynote">Book this keynote</a></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Assessment />

  <section class="dark">
    <div class="wrap split">
      <div>
        <h2>Why Jack</h2>
        <p class="lede" style="color:#E3D5BE">He has sat in the rooms where technology decisions get made, defended and paid for.</p>
        <p>Across 25 years in risk and advisory, 15 of them at the Big Four, Jack noticed one pattern: the leaders who struggled most were not the least talented. They were the least equipped to make a call and stand behind it under pressure. Most of them were waiting for a control that did not exist, and fear filled the gap.</p>
        <p>That observation became a method. The method became one keynote, built for two rooms, and the same questions he asks a board when a new system is about to go live.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid c2 stats">
        <div class="card"><p class="stat"><strong>25</strong>Years in risk and advisory, inside the rooms where technology decisions carry the highest cost.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><p class="stat"><strong>15</strong>Years at the Big Four as a former Big 4 director, building the teams that found the gaps before the regulator did.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><p class="stat"><strong>$10B</strong>Health system where Jack served as acting enterprise risk and audit leader, reporting to the C-suite and audit committee.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><p class="stat"><strong>$2.5M</strong>Board-mandated cybersecurity program for a $4B health system. Urgent gaps closed within one year.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>The audience leaves with</h2>
      <p class="lede">Four things that outlast the applause.</p>
      <div class="grid c2" style="margin-top:1.5rem">
        <div class="card">
          <p class="kicker">01</p>
          <h3>A method, not a feeling.</h3>
          <p>Treat new technology like a new hire. A job description, a manager, a probation period, a review. Every person in the room can apply it to the next tool, vendor or system that lands on their desk.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <p class="kicker">02</p>
          <h3>A name for what has been slowing them down.</h3>
          <p>Nobody owns the decision, so fear makes it. Once a room can say that out loud, it can assign the decision. This keynote gives them the language.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <p class="kicker">03</p>
          <h3>A contrarian view on risk they will argue about on the way home.</h3>
          <p>Governance has a reputation for slowing things down, and sometimes it earns it. Fear is slower, and nobody can test it. The risk person in the room is the one saying go, on conditions.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <p class="kicker">04</p>
          <h3>Something they walk out ready to teach.</h3>
          <p>The three questions are short enough to repeat without a slide. An executive who attended should be able to walk a direct report through them the next morning.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="tight">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>How it is delivered</h2>
      <p class="lede">Three formats. One standard.</p>
      <p>Every format starts with a 30-minute discovery call, so the talk is built for the room and the decision in front of it.</p>
      <div class="grid c3" style="margin-top:1.5rem">
        <div class="card accent">
          <p class="kicker">45 to 60 min</p>
          <h3>Keynote</h3>
          <p>The full talk with live audience exercises. For conferences, leadership summits and association events.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card accent">
          <p class="kicker">Keynote plus half day</p>
          <h3>Keynote and workshop</h3>
          <p>The keynote, then a working session where teams apply the new-hire method to a technology decision they are facing now.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card accent">
          <p class="kicker">45 to 60 min</p>
          <h3>Virtual keynote</h3>
          <p>Live on Zoom or Teams with interactive Q&amp;A, rebuilt for the screen. For distributed leadership teams and association audiences.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="tight">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="cta-band">
        <h2>The next step</h2>
        <p>The right talk, in the right room, to change what the audience does when they get back to work.</p>
        <div class="btns" style="justify-content:center;margin-top:.4rem"><a class="btn" href={site.calendly} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Book a discovery call</a></div>
        <p class="meta" style="margin-top:1.2rem">Jack replies within one business day and asks questions before he sends a proposal, starting with who is in the room and what decision they are facing.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

</Base>

<style>
  .pull { max-width: 48rem; font-size: clamp(1.4rem, 2.6vw, 1.9rem); margin: 0; }
  .stats .card { padding: 1.3rem; }
  .stat { margin: 0; }
  .stat strong { display: block; font-family: var(--font-head); font-size: 2.4rem; line-height: 1; color: var(--orange-bright); margin-bottom: .5rem; }
  .dark .btns { margin-top: 2rem; }
</style>
